exit command is a built-in shell utility used to close the current terminal session, terminate a shell script, or exit from a logged-in user environment.

exit [status]

The status argument is an optional integer between 0 and 255 known as the exit status code or return code.
exit 0: Signals Success. Everything ran cleanly with zero errors. (This is the default value if you type exit on its own).
exit 1 to exit 255: Signals an Error. Different numbers indicate different types of failures (e.g., file not found, permission denied).

to check exit status of last command use : echo $?

echo $SHLVL
it will tell you the shell level you are using in temrinal
if echo $SHLVL is 4 you are inside 4th sub-process

process->sub-process->sub-sub-process->sub-sub-sub-process
use exit  or ctrl+D to exit(terminate) from sub-process or process to its parent process

3 Main Use Cases

1. Ending a Terminal or SSH Session
(Alternatively, you can press Ctrl + D on a blank command line; it sends an End-of-File marker that acts exactly like running the exit command).

2. Stepping out of a Subshell or User Switch (su)
root@server:/# exit
exit
ec2-user@server:~$ 


3. Terminating Shell Scripts Safely
exit command inside the script terminates the executing script 

#!/bin/bash

# Check if a critical configuration file exists
if [ ! -f "/etc/app.conf" ]; then
    echo "❌ Error: Configuration file missing!"
    exit 1 # Aborts the script immediately and reports an error
fi

echo "File found. Proceeding with application..."
exit 0 # Script finished perfectly


